<p>After a hail or windstorm, most San Marcos property owners meet one adjuster: the person the insurance company sends. Some later bring in a second one, a public adjuster, and wonder how the two fit together. The difference between a public adjuster vs. an insurance adjuster comes down to a simple question: who does each one work for? This guide gives a practical, side-by-side look at both roles on a typical Central Texas storm claim, including how each is paid, what each does, and the Texas rules that apply.</p>

<p>We are a <a href="/">San Marcos public adjuster</a>, so we have an interest in this topic, and we will say that up front. Our goal here is to explain the roles clearly so you can decide what your claim needs. Plenty of claims are resolved fairly without a public adjuster. If you want a broader overview of the different adjuster types, see <a href="/what-is-a-public-claims-adjuster/">What Is a Public Claims Adjuster?</a></p>

<h2>Who each adjuster works for</h2>

<p><strong>The insurance adjuster</strong> works for the insurance company. That person might be a <em>staff adjuster</em> employed by the insurer or an <em>independent adjuster</em> the insurer hires on contract, which is common after large storms when claim volume spikes. Either way, the insurer assigns them, directs their work, and relies on their findings to decide the claim. Their role is to investigate the loss, apply the policy, and write an estimate for the company.</p>

<p><strong>The public adjuster</strong> works for you, the policyholder. You choose whether to hire one, and the relationship is set out in a written contract. A public adjuster documents the damage, reviews the policy, prepares an estimate of the loss, presents the claim to the insurer, and handles communication on your behalf. Texas requires public adjuster contracts to state plainly, "WE REPRESENT THE INSURED ONLY."</p>

<p>Neither role is inherently good or bad. Many insurance adjusters are careful and fair. The point is that each one has a different client, and that shapes what they focus on.</p>

<h2>How each is paid</h2>

<p>The insurer pays its own adjusters through salary, per-claim fees, or contract arrangements. You never receive a bill from the insurance company’s adjuster.</p>

<p>A public adjuster is paid by the policyholder, usually as a percentage of the claim payment. Texas law limits that compensation to no more than 10% of the insurance settlement on the claim (Texas Insurance Code §4102.104). There is an important exception: if the insurer pays, or commits in writing to pay, the policy limits within 72 hours after the loss is reported, the public adjuster cannot take a percentage fee and is limited to reasonable hourly compensation. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins.</p>

<h2>Side-by-side comparison</h2>

<table>
