<tr><td>Can do repairs</td><td>Not their role</td><td>Prohibited on claims they adjust</td></tr>
<tr><td>Legal advice</td><td>No</td><td>No; refers legal questions to an attorney</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>What each adjuster actually does on a claim</h2>

<h3>The insurance adjuster’s duties</h3>
<ul>
<li>Contacts you after the claim is reported and schedules an inspection.</li>
<li>Inspects the property and takes photos and measurements.</li>
<li>Reviews the policy, including deductibles, exclusions, and endorsements.</li>
<li>Writes an estimate, often in Xactimate, and may request engineer or specialist reports.</li>
<li>Requests information from you, such as photos, receipts, or a proof of loss.</li>
<li>Recommends whether and how much the insurer should pay.</li>
</ul>

<h3>The public adjuster’s duties</h3>
<ul>
<li>Reviews your policy and explains, in plain terms, what coverages and limits may apply.</li>
<li>Inspects and documents the damage independently, including interior, exterior, and contents where relevant.</li>
<li>Prepares a detailed estimate of the loss.</li>
<li>Answers the insurer’s information requests and keeps the file organized.</li>
<li>Meets the insurer’s adjuster on site and discusses differences in scope or price.</li>
<li>Prepares <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">supplements</a> and, where the policy allows and you choose it, helps with the <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">appraisal</a> process.</li>
</ul>

