<h2>How they interact on a San Marcos hail or wind claim</h2>

<p>Storm claims are where this comparison matters most for local owners. The NOAA Storm Events Database lists 180 hail reports and 109 thunderstorm wind reports in Hays County, with hail peaking in May and April. Hays County is not in the TWIA catastrophe area, so most San Marcos properties carry wind and hail coverage through their regular homeowners or commercial insurer. Here is how a typical claim might unfold when both adjusters are involved.</p>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/stock/adjuster-roof-inspection-policyholder.webp" alt="Adjuster inspecting a hail-damaged roof while the homeowner looks on" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>On many storm claims, the insurer’s adjuster and the policyholder’s representative inspect the same roof.</figcaption></figure>

<ol>
<li><strong>The storm and the report.</strong> After an event like the May 9, 2024 storm, when NWS recorded 2.5-inch hail near the San Marcos outlet mall and 70 mph gusts that damaged roofs (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1181414" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NOAA NCEI event record</a>), you report the claim to your insurer.</li>
<li><strong>The insurer’s inspection.</strong> The insurer assigns a staff or independent adjuster, who inspects the roof and exterior and writes an estimate.</li>
<li><strong>Hiring a public adjuster (optional).</strong> You may hire a public adjuster before or after that inspection. Once hired, the public adjuster notifies the insurer in writing and becomes your point of contact.</li>
<li><strong>Joint or follow-up inspection.</strong> The two adjusters often meet on the roof. The public adjuster points out damage, measurements, and items the first estimate did not include, such as damaged metal roof panels, flashing, vents, gutters, screens, or HVAC fins.</li>
<li><strong>Estimate comparison.</strong> The public adjuster submits a detailed estimate and supporting photos. The insurer’s adjuster reviews it and may revise the insurer’s estimate, request more information, or explain disagreements.</li>
<li><strong>Resolution.</strong> Most claims end with a negotiated scope and payment. If the disagreement is only about the amount of covered loss, the policy may allow appraisal. If coverage itself is disputed, an attorney may be needed.</li>
</ol>

<p>Throughout, the insurer still controls the decision and must follow Texas Prompt Payment rules in <a href="https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/IN/htm/IN.542.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Insurance Code Chapter 542</a>. Hiring a public adjuster does not change those deadlines, and it does not guarantee a higher payment.</p>

<h3>Local roof details that often come up</h3>
<p>San Marcos subdivisions are dominated by composition shingle roofs, while standing-seam and exposed-fastener metal roofs are common on Hill Country homes, barns, and outbuildings west of I-35. Each raises different questions on a storm claim. Shingle roofs bring up hail-hit counts, test squares, and accessories. Metal roofs often turn on whether dents are treated as covered damage or as cosmetic under a policy endorsement. Reading the policy language for those details is a shared task: the insurer’s adjuster applies it, and a public adjuster can explain it to you and respond to it.</p>

<h2>Texas rules for public adjusters (Chapter 4102)</h2>

<p>Public adjusters in Texas are licensed and regulated by the Texas Department of Insurance under <a href="https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/IN/htm/IN.4102.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Texas Insurance Code Chapter 4102</a>. Key protections for policyholders include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Licensing.</strong> A public adjuster must hold a Texas public adjuster license. You can verify a license through TDI. Ours is #[[license]], held through [[company]].</li>
<li><strong>Written contract on approved terms.</strong> The contract must use TDI-approved terms and display "WE REPRESENT THE INSURED ONLY."</li>
<li><strong>72-hour right to rescind.</strong> You may cancel within 72 hours of signing.</li>
<li><strong>Fee cap.</strong> Compensation may not exceed 10% of the insurance settlement, with the 72-hour policy-limits exception described above.</li>
<li><strong>No repair work.</strong> A public adjuster may not take part in repairing or rebuilding the property on a claim they adjust. Texas law also does not allow your contractor to act as your public adjuster.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Which one do you need?</h2>

<p>You will always deal with the insurance adjuster. The real question is whether you also want someone working only for you. Many owners handle small, straightforward claims themselves. A public adjuster is more often worth considering when the damage is large or complicated, the estimate seems to leave out obvious work, the claim has stalled, or you simply do not have time to manage the process. Our guide <a href="/do-i-need-a-public-adjuster/">Do I Need a Public Adjuster?</a> walks through that decision, and our <a href="/services/hail-damage-claims/">hail damage claims</a> and <a href="/services/wind-damage-claims/">wind damage claims</a> pages explain how we handle storm losses.</p>

<p>If you would like a no-cost opinion on where your claim stands, a San Marcos public adjuster can look over your policy and the insurer’s estimate. Request a <a href="/free-claim-review/">Free Claim Review</a> or call [[phone]]. We serve San Marcos, Hays County, and surrounding Central Texas communities.</p>
